WEBVTT

00:00:00.550 --> 00:00:04.975
An employee wants to understand how to purchase equipment for their team.

00:00:04.925 --> 00:00:09.388
The answer is scattered across documents, email, and colleagues.

00:00:09.388 --> 00:00:12.325
Finding a paragraph is only the start.

00:00:12.325 --> 00:00:14.038
Is it current?

00:00:14.038 --> 00:00:16.550
Is the employee allowed to see it?

00:00:16.550 --> 00:00:18.975
And who approves the next step?

00:00:20.633 --> 00:00:25.221
Follow an illustrative use case: an internal purchasing assistant.

00:00:25.171 --> 00:00:29.833
An employee asks a question in an application built with AI Studio.

00:00:29.833 --> 00:00:36.933
The application finds the relevant procedure, helps draft a request, and passes it to a responsible reviewer.

00:00:36.933 --> 00:00:42.721
This is a process you can design, not a built-in purchasing system for every organization.

00:00:44.383 --> 00:00:49.608
Inputs may include documents or images that need OCR to extract text.

00:00:49.558 --> 00:00:55.883
NER helps identify entities such as personal names, alongside policy-based PII rules.

00:00:55.883 --> 00:01:01.246
Detection is assistance, not a guarantee that every sensitive detail will be found.

00:01:01.246 --> 00:01:05.333
Configure processing, chunking, and indexing next.

00:01:05.333 --> 00:01:14.233
Folders, synchronized sources, knowledge pipelines, hybrid retrieval, and reranking help organize the collection and test its quality.

00:01:15.883 --> 00:01:21.183
Before a document becomes part of an answer, the caller needs appropriate access.

00:01:21.133 --> 00:01:26.733
Organization boundaries, departments, and sensitivity levels affect retrieval.

00:01:26.733 --> 00:01:30.346
Unauthorized documents should not enter the context.

00:01:30.346 --> 00:01:36.158
In this example, the assistant retrieves the permitted passages needed to prepare the next step.

00:01:37.842 --> 00:01:40.692
A workflow is not a fixed path.

00:01:40.642 --> 00:01:48.292
Connect node families for inputs, retrieval, models, branches, loops, data processing, and outputs.

00:01:48.292 --> 00:01:53.854
Define input variables, run a test, and inspect each step to find problems.

00:01:53.854 --> 00:02:02.267
Drafts, version history, DSL import and export, and reusable graph snippets support deliberate changes before publication.

00:02:03.925 --> 00:02:08.425
With context ready, the application still needs a route to a model.

00:02:08.375 --> 00:02:13.975
In this diagram, AI Gateway describes the model orchestration layer in AI Studio.

00:02:13.975 --> 00:02:22.400
It filters eligible models by policy, manages connection credentials, and can fall back to an allowed model after a suitable error.

00:02:22.400 --> 00:02:27.650
Both internal and external models must meet the configuration and data policy.

00:02:29.342 --> 00:02:32.304
Data protection takes several layers.

00:02:32.254 --> 00:02:37.929
On the chat path, personal information can be masked according to the configured policy.

00:02:37.929 --> 00:02:42.404
Sensitivity labels determine whether an external provider is allowed.

00:02:42.404 --> 00:02:45.929
Tool calls also pass destination controls.

00:02:45.929 --> 00:02:48.904
A disallowed request must be blocked.

00:02:48.904 --> 00:02:54.254
Masking personal information alone does not make every payload safe to send outside.

00:02:55.925 --> 00:03:01.138
For custom logic, a Code node sends work to a separate sandbox service.

00:03:01.087 --> 00:03:07.325
This execution boundary separates user code from the main process and applies the sandbox limits.

00:03:07.325 --> 00:03:11.112
The node stays disabled when the service is not configured.

00:03:11.112 --> 00:03:17.850
A sandbox does not replace permissions or outbound controls; tool calls still need their own policies.

00:03:19.508 --> 00:03:22.096
Back to the purchasing request.

00:03:22.046 --> 00:03:26.058
AI prepares a draft for a responsible person to review.

00:03:26.058 --> 00:03:29.408
If changes are needed, it returns for revision.

00:03:29.408 --> 00:03:34.408
Only after approval does this designed workflow continue to the configured tool.

00:03:34.408 --> 00:03:41.608
Build this gate for actions with consequences; do not assume that every model output should be executed automatically.

00:03:43.258 --> 00:03:49.758
Connect built-in tools, API tools, MCP servers, or a workflow exposed as a tool.

00:03:49.708 --> 00:03:55.496
Data source integrations and external knowledge services bring in information from other systems.

00:03:55.496 --> 00:03:59.871
Moderation extensions require a compatible service contract.

00:03:59.871 --> 00:04:07.583
Enable only the operations you need and verify access, connection settings, and permitted destinations before use.

00:04:09.258 --> 00:04:18.308
After testing, publish an application to the web, embed a widget, or integrate through API and MCP access with appropriate permissions.

00:04:18.258 --> 00:04:21.721
Messaging channels need provider-specific setup.

00:04:21.721 --> 00:04:28.033
Scheduled triggers and webhooks can start workflows with suitable authentication and event configuration.

00:04:28.033 --> 00:04:34.933
API documentation, access keys, and quotas help manage how other systems call the application.

00:04:36.592 --> 00:04:42.554
Configure conversation starters, inputs, follow-up questions, and business behavior.

00:04:42.504 --> 00:04:48.842
Long-term memory includes evidence review, approval, and retention settings tied to a subject.

00:04:48.842 --> 00:04:54.842
Speech recognition, text-to-speech, and image generation depend on connected models.

00:04:54.842 --> 00:05:00.654
Enable the capabilities that serve the use case, and review what the application retains.

00:05:02.342 --> 00:05:06.617
Inspect conversations and run logs to understand what happened.

00:05:06.567 --> 00:05:13.167
Use evaluation datasets, compatible run comparisons, and annotations to check quality.

00:05:13.167 --> 00:05:16.792
Monitor usage and operations to detect problems.

00:05:16.792 --> 00:05:25.554
Data can be prepared for training, while fine-tuning still depends on a runner, resources, and deployment configuration, not just a button.

00:05:27.258 --> 00:05:32.983
Manage members, invitations, roles, keys, and quotas at workspace level.

00:05:32.933 --> 00:05:38.983
Identity, data protection, model providers, and S3 storage support operations.

00:05:38.983 --> 00:05:46.808
Instance administrators have separate access to organization and account views, service health, and runtime configuration.

00:05:46.808 --> 00:05:53.608
Some network and feature-flag screens are read-only inspection surfaces, not direct editing controls.

00:05:55.300 --> 00:06:04.600
The same building blocks can support internal policy questions, assist customer support staff, process documents, or coordinate business approvals.

00:06:04.550 --> 00:06:11.400
Each application needs its own data sources, access rules, tools, and evaluation criteria.

00:06:11.400 --> 00:06:14.612
Test within a small scope before expanding.

00:06:16.300 --> 00:06:21.188
AI Studio connects people, knowledge, models, and workflows.

00:06:21.138 --> 00:06:24.113
The value is more than a faster answer.

00:06:24.113 --> 00:06:31.688
It is a process you can inspect: where the data came from, which model was called, who made the decision, and what happened.

00:06:31.688 --> 00:06:37.888
Start with a concrete use case, evaluate quality, and observe operations before expanding.
